    "datePosted": "2026-11-06T11:04:03.000Z",
    "description": "JOB PURPOSE The HIV/TB Lead provides technical leadership for the design, quality and results of HIV and tuberculosis services across the FCI hub-and-spoke network. Reporting to the Technical Director, the role ensures HIV and TB care is delivered as part of one integrated, five-disease client encounter at community spokes and Circle of Hope posts; drives case-finding, linkage, retention and viral suppression through faith-anchored community platforms and the Health Extension Programme; and aligns all FCI HIV/TB approaches with FMoH national guidelines. The Lead also provides technical oversight of malaria case-management quality as a secondary responsibility. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES 30% - Project Leadership and Coordination Lead overall planning, implementation, monitoring, and reporting of project activities. Develop annual work plans, procurement plans, and implementation schedules. Coordinate project implementation across all target districts. Supervise Project Officers and facilitate performance management. Ensure timely delivery of project outputs and outcomes. HIV and TB services delivered to national standard at every functional spoke and integrated within a single client encounter; current technical guidance issued and demonstrably in use across all ten networks. Improved case-finding yield and male reach; ART and TPT initiation and retention improved; viral suppression trending toward the 95% target; loss to follow-up measurably reduced. 20% - Technical Quality Assurance Ensure quality implementation of: Support adaptation and contextualization of project materials. Ensure safeguarding, disability inclusion, gender, and conflict sensitivity standards are maintained. Frontline and facility workers demonstrably competent in integrated HIV/TB service delivery; mentorship functioning to schedule with documented competency verification at each cascade level. Confirmed malaria cases treated to national standard; case-management quality gaps identified and corrected through the mentorship system. Cross-cutting standards demonstrably applied in HIV/TB services; integration maintained across the three technical portfolios. 15% - Leadership, Governance, Partnership & System Integration Stronger coordination and collaboration between FCI, FMoH, RHBs, Zonal/Woreda Health Offices, FBOs, facilities, and communities. FCI interventions are aligned with national policies, strategies, standards, guidelines, and government priorities. FCI HIV/TB approaches endorsed by and embedded within national systems; no parallel structures created; a coordinated and non-duplicative partner landscape in supported regions. 15% - Monitoring, Evaluation, Accountability and Learning HIV/TB performance actively managed from routine data; operational research questions answered to schedule; evidence package supports the Year 1 go/no-go decision. Work closely with the MEAL Specialist to ensure timely data collection. Ensure implementation of baseline, outcome survey, and endline evaluations. Support documentation of lessons learned, case studies, and success stories. Work closely with the MEAL Specialist to ensure timely data collection. Ensure implementation of baseline, outcome survey, and endline evaluations Lead quarterly learning reviews. Review indicator progress and adaptive management actions. Support documentation of lessons learned, case studies, and success stories. 10% Grant, Finance and Compliance Management Monitor project expenditure against approved budgets. Ensure donor compliance and WV grant standards. Prepare quality programmatic reports. Coordinate audits and project reviews. FCI resources are strategically directed toward priority health-system gaps and high-impact interventions. Improved value for money and reduced duplication across technical and operational activities. Greater integration of FCI investments into government and FBO plans, systems, and structures. Sustainable methods are established for functions initially supported by the project. Increased government and FBO capacity to maintain key interventions beyond project funding. Reduced dependence on parallel project-funded systems. 10% - Documentation and Knowledge Management Produce quarterly reports. Develop learning briefs and success stories. Support dissemination of project evidence and learning products. Document HIV/TB achievements, challenges, lessons learned, and good practices. Prepare technical reports, progress updates, and donor inputs. Develop case studies, success stories, learning briefs, and technical summaries. Document effective approaches in HIV case finding, ART, viral suppression, TB screening, treatment, and prevention. Compile and synthesize HIV/TB program data and evidence for decision-making. Document quality-improvement initiatives and results. Capture lessons from community, facility, and referral-system interventions. Maintain organized technical guidelines, tools, reports, and reference materials. Coordinate with MEAL teams to ensure program evidence is accurately documented. Facilitate learning and reflection sessions and document key actions. Share promising practices and lessons with government, partners, health facilities, and project teams. Support preparation of presentations, review-meeting materials, and knowledge products. Ensuring lessons learned are used for program adaptation and continuous improvement. Support dissemination and scale-up of effective HIV/TB practices and innovations",
